=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is that the ospfd daemon on router_core_1 has been killed or has crashed,
even though the persistent configuration file (/etc/frr/frr.conf) contains a fully
correct OSPF setup (router-id 172.16.0.0; networks 172.16.0.0/31 area 0,
172.16.0.2/31 area 0, 172.16.0.6/31 area 1). With ospfd not running, router_core_1
does not originate any OSPF LSAs and does not install any OSPF-learned routes into
its RIB. This breaks connectivity in both directions: forward traffic from
host_1_1_1_1 arriving at router_core_1 via switch_dist_1_1 is black-holed because
router_core_1 has no route to 10.200.0.0/24 (the server farm); return traffic from
the server farm is dropped at router_core_3 and router_core_2 because neither has
a route to 10.1.1.0/24 or 172.16.0.6/31 (the prefixes that router_core_1 would
normally advertise). router_core_2's OSPF neighbor table confirms the adjacency to
router_core_1 (172.16.0.0/172.16.0.1) is completely absent. All three physical
interfaces on router_core_1 (eth0, eth1, eth2) are UP/LOWER_UP, definitively ruling
out the physical cable fault the networking team suspected. The root cause is that
the ospfd process stopped on router_core_1 while the frr.conf remained intact.
The corrective fix is to restart the FRR stack on router_core_1 (e.g.
`service frr restart` inside the container); since frr.conf is correct, ospfd will
re-start, re-form adjacencies with router_core_2, router_core_3, and switch_dist_1_1,
and the full routing table will converge within seconds.
=== END ===
